INTERPRETATION
Compared with the user guesses: public keep-alive median inference 0.121 ms versus 1 ms predicted; median round trip 37.007642 ms versus 250 ms predicted. The differences are -0.879 ms and -212.992358 ms respectively.
No-keepalive median RTT 254.907023 ms is 6.89 times the keep-alive median. Reopening connections adds TCP/TLS/HTTP setup cost; these aggregate measurements do not isolate individual protocol costs. Server work remains sub-millisecond for typical requests.
Inside-Codespace localhost median RTT 1.217944 ms removes the public tunnel and laptop network path, though framework/socket/client overhead remains. Its first request had 419.785 ms in inference and a 423.924687 ms RTT; warm median inference was 0.086 ms. This is consistent with first-use overhead, but no profile isolates its exact cause. The public runs occurred later against the already warmed process; their first requests are connection-first observations, not fresh-model cold starts.
Pre-run expectation supported: network/framework/client residual dominates the representative public request (36.859641993/37.007641993 ms, 99.60%). Inference is the largest internal server stage in the warm medians.
Representative timing components from actual request index 64: {"parse": 0.019, "normalise": 0.018, "inference": 0.092, "build": 0.011, "server_other": 0.008000000000000007, "network_and_framework": 36.85964199311379}; sum=37.00764199311379 ms, equal to measured RTT. Diagram: timing_breakdown.png. Separate marginal medians were not added together.
400 identical distinct Team 25 readings: sequential singles 37113.547014000 ms; one batch 181.528824993 ms; measured speedup 204.449883x. Two separate persistent sessions were each warmed by one one-row request, with handshake/warm-up/file I/O outside timers. Singles were sequential and ran first; batch ran second. One trial per arm, so no confidence interval or stable speedup claim.
Batch amortises repeated network/framework work and executes the network on a matrix. All 400 outputs matched in order within atol=0.01 days, rtol=0; maximum observed difference was 0.009999999999999787 days, one API rounding unit. Different batched float32 accumulation can move a prediction across a rounding boundary. The separate 50-reading check observed exact equality (0 days difference).
Actual 400-reading inputs, original source indices, per-single timings, batch response and setup are saved under comparison_400/. Synthetic 200-request benchmark inputs are generated by the untouched supplied client as required; all data-file analysis and the 400-reading sample use Team 25 only.

